            "label": "What is in it"
          }
        },
        "q_why_do_you_publish_the_whole_formulation": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why do you publish the whole formulation?",
            "answer": "<p>So you can see exactly what you are putting on your skin, and how much of each thing. Every product page carries every ingredient and its exact percentage, in a table, each one linked to a page explaining what it is doing and what the evidence behind it says.</p><p>An ingredient list tells you what is in a product. The percentage tells you how much.</p>",
            "set_piece": "none",
            "ethos_icon": "ethos-formulation",
            "ethos_label": "Every formulation published in full"
          }
        },
        "q_does_it_have_a_scent": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does it have a scent?",
            "answer": "<p>A faint earthy one, from a single ingredient. Vetiver root oil is a natural essential oil distilled from the roots of a grass, and every product carries it at a tenth of a percent.</p><p>There is no synthetic fragrance in the range, and no \"parfum\" catch-all standing in for components we have not named. If your skin reacts easily, patch test before first use.</p>",
            "set_piece": "none",
            "ethos_icon": "none",
            "ethos_label": ""
          }
        },
        "q_what_keeps_it_from_going_off": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What keeps it from going off?",
            "answer": "<p>Two things keep each bottle sound. The first is the preservative, a ready-made blend of sodium benzoate and gluconolactone. Sodium benzoate does the preserving, and gluconolactone helps it work. The second is the pH. Sodium citrate buffers the cream at pH 5 to 5.5, close to the skin's own. At the end of making, the pH is checked and fine-tuned with a little sodium hydroxide or citric acid.</p><p>A water-based cream with nothing preserving it can grow mould and bacteria within days.</p>",
            "set_piece": "none",
            "ethos_icon": "none",
            "ethos_label": ""
          }
        },
        "q_is_it_vegan_and_is_it_tested_on_animals": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is it vegan, and is it tested on animals?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes, the range is vegan. Every ingredient is plant, mineral or ferment derived, or made in the lab as an exact match for one that is, and nothing in it comes from animals. Testing cosmetics on animals has been banned in the UK since 1998, and the only person our products are tested on is the chemist who makes them.</p><p>We hold no certification for either at the moment.</p>",
            "set_piece": "none",
            "ethos_icon": "ethos-cruelty",
            "ethos_label": "Vegan and cruelty-free"
          }
        },
        "q_is_it_organic": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is it organic?",
            "answer": "<p>Many of the oils are, and they are marked on every formulation table. A star means organic, a caret means cold-pressed, and most of the plant oils carry both. A hash means upcycled, made from a by-product of another process.</p> <p>The finished products are not certified organic. Certification covers the whole product and the whole process, and we have not been through it.</p>",
            "set_piece": "organic_key",
            "ethos_icon": "none",
            "ethos_label": ""
          }
        },
        "q_what_is_all_the_microbiome_talk_about": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is all the microbiome talk about?",
            "answer": "<p>Your skin carries its own community of bacteria and fungi, and the research suggests skin does better when that community is supported rather than stripped.</p><p>Two ingredients are there for it. Inulin, a plant prebiotic, feeds the microbes already on your skin in laboratory studies, and a chlorella extract has supplier evidence for holding back the less welcome species while sparing the helpful ones. Beyond those, nothing in the formula is there to strip, and every finished product is adjusted to slightly acidic (pH 5 to 5.5), the level the skin's residents prefer. Of everything here, that has the strongest evidence behind it.</p><p>Skin microbiome science is young, and much of it is still in cell culture rather than on faces. The <a href=\"/pages/the-skin-microbiome\">skin microbiome page</a> sets out what the evidence supports and where it runs out.</p>",
            "set_piece": "none",
            "ethos_icon": "ethos-microbiome",
            "ethos_label": "Made for your microbiome",
            "caveat": "<p>We review the published research rather than run our own, and claim only what it supports. <a href=\"/pages/our-science\">Read the science</a>.</p>"
          }
        },
